  "query_preview": "..\nMonday through Wednesday: High pressure has moved into the \nregion bringing increased potential for fog and low stratus for \nthe Columbia Basin and Spokane area Monday morning. Once the low\nclouds and fog dissipate late Monday morning, the INW will be \nleft with slowly increasing high clouds in the afternoon. \nTemperatures will be near normal in the upper 40s to mid 50s. A \nshortwave trough moves near the northern California coast \nTuesday morning, PWATs increase to 120-160% of normal across \ncentral and eastern Washington, bringing a small chance of \nsprinkles Tuesday morning. Weak high pressure stays in place \nthrough Tuesday night into Wednesday leading to dry conditions, \nbut changes arrive Wednesday night.\n\nThursday and Friday: A pair of systems will move through the \nregion early Thursday and Friday, bringing periods of high \nmountain snow and very light valley rain. The best chance to get\nsnow down to the mountain passes is Thursday night in the \nCascades but it is not a guarantee. Snow levels are forecasted \nto be in the 4000-5000 foot range overnight Thursday. Thursday \nnight into Friday is working with more impressive PWATs around \n150-200% which brings a higher chance of rain making it into the\nvalleys. The cold front passage Friday will bring breezy \nsouthwest winds gusting around 20-30 mph across the Columbia \nBasin, Palouse, and Spokane/Cda metro. From 4 PM Thursday to 4 \nPM Friday there is a 10% chance of 4 inches or more of snow at \nStevens Pass and a 30% chance at Washington Pass. \n\nSaturday through Monday: There is generally good agreement of\nridging returning behind this pair of systems into the weekend\nbut not all the ensemble clusters are dry. A deep surface low\nwill form in the Gulf of Alaska, drawing in moisture from\nsubtropical origins. There is about a 50/50 split in the global\nensembles that brings the longwave trough closer to the Pacific\nnorthwest which would favor rain and high mountain snow in the\neast slopes of the Cascades, northeast WA, and north ID.\nSoutheast Alaska and British Columbia coastlines are most\nfavored to receive rain and high mountain snow from this, but\nany eastward track of the longwave and the shortwave responsible\nfor the deepening surface low will bring the moisture plume more\ntowards the Pacific NW. /DB",
